WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.880
Ridley Scott's sci-fi opus Blade Runner has
become known as one of the best, most influential

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.230
movies of all time.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.179
One of the movie's best lines, however, didn't
come from screenwriters.

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:13.110
Rather, it came from the actor playing the
role: Rutger Hauer.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:18.380
Hauer plays Roy, the leader of a band of "replicants"
— basically bioengineered androids created

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.590
for slave labor.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:24.849
To prevent a full-on revolt, scientists give
replicants a limited four-year lifespan.

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:28.839
Roy spends the movie trying desperately to
find a "cure" for the design flaw dooming

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:31.060
him and his friends to short lives.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.090
By the end of the film, he's given up hope,
and spares the Blade Runner, Deckard — played

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.480
by Harrison Ford — hired to kill him.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.320
During a table read of the script, while reciting
his final monologue, Rutger Hauer added a

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.320
final coda:

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:52.000
"All those moments will be lost in time...like
tears in the rain."

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.899
The line was so-well received that it was
added to the script, and it has since become

00:00:55.899 --> 00:00:58.690
one of the most quoted bits from the entire
movie.

